{#
  The one layout. Masthead, version status, footer, and nothing else — every page's
  structure is its own, because seven pages do not need an abstraction and a partial that
  exists to be reused twice is harder to read than the two copies it replaced.

  ── The two stylesheets, and why they are two ──────────────────────────────────────

  `assetBase` points a published version's stylesheet, fonts and favicon at version-scoped
  copies taken at publication, so a colour change in 2028 cannot restyle a methodology
  document that scorecards have been issued against. That is R2.

  `chromeHref` is the other half, and it is new on 2026-08-12. It is a derived,
  fingerprinted, chrome-only sheet — masthead, status strip and footer, every selector
  anchored to one of those — linked by **every** page including a frozen one. That is R1:
  a chrome change reaches every address on the site without a version event.

  The two are in disjoint scopes by construction rather than by discipline. The chrome
  sheet declares no `:root` (two `:root` blocks merge rather than isolate, and the frozen
  `<main>` would silently take the live palette while every byte check stayed green) and
  no `@font-face` (families are document-scoped, so the chrome reuses faces the page has
  already loaded — declaring them again would fetch a second copy of four byte-identical
  fonts and put the page 119.6 KiB over its 150 KiB budget). Order matters: the frozen
  sheet is linked first and governs `<main>`; the chrome sheet is linked second and cannot
  reach inside it. Check 27b asserts that against the rendered DOM.
#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en-GB">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ title }}</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{ description }}">
{%- if canonical %}
<link rel="canonical" href="https://{{ site.domain }}{{ canonical }}">
{%- endif %}
<link rel="icon" href="{{ assetBase | default('') }}/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
{#
  Archivo is font-display: optional, so a cold visit either has it inside the
  browser's block period or spends the whole page load in Arial Narrow and never
  swaps. Preloading it makes the first outcome the likely one — which matters
  because the wordmark and the measure's labels are Archivo, and §13 item 4 says
  the hero measure is one of two things holding a sceptical reader past forty
  seconds. Same origin, so check 6 is untouched.
#}
<link rel="preload" href="{{ assetBase | default('') }}/fonts/archivo-subset.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ assetBase | default('') }}/styles.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ chromeHref }}">
</head>
<body>
<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

<header class="masthead">
  <div class="sheet">
    <a class="wordmark" href="/"{% if nav == "home" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>ordoia</a>
    <nav aria-label="Primary">
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/oal/"{% if nav == "oal" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>the rubric</a></li>
        <li><a href="/services/"{% if nav == "services" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>services</a></li>
        <li><a href="/independence/"{% if nav == "independence" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>independence</a></li>
        <li><a href="/about/"{% if nav == "about" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>about</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>
{%- if versionOf %}
{%- set vrec = oal.versions | versionRecord(versionOf) %}
{#
  The version status stamp. Ruled by the user 2026-08-12: status renders live on every
  version page and updates whenever a valid version revision occurs.

  It is a fact ABOUT the document, not part of it, and that is the line the freeze cuts
  on: the rubric's words are frozen, the label describing that version's standing is not.
  So it sits here, in the chrome, OUTSIDE `<main>` — deliberately, and this is the only
  cheap moment to draw that boundary. Inside `<main>` it would freeze with the content,
  and moving it later would mean editing published bytes: a version event over a label.

  `/oal/v1.0/` saying `Current` after v1.1 publishes is a published claim becoming false
  by mechanism. A superseded stamp names and links its successor, because a reader who
  lands on an old version from a search result must be one click from the current one —
  that is the whole job of the stamp. Check 29 holds all of it to oal.json.

  The separator is a template-emitted text node, never `::before`. Generated content is
  not a DOM text node, so check 7 cannot measure its contrast — which is how a separator
  shipped at 1.37:1 on this project (CHANGES.md row 49).
#}
<p class="vstatus" data-version="{{ vrec.version }}" data-status="{{ vrec.status }}">
  <span class="v">{{ oal.name }} v{{ vrec.version }}</span><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>{% if vrec.supersededBy %}<span class="s">Superseded by <a href="/oal/v{{ vrec.supersededBy }}/">v{{ vrec.supersededBy }}</a></span>{% else %}<span class="s">{{ vrec.status }}</span>{% endif %}
</p>
{%- endif %}

{% if versionOf and (versionOf | isFrozen) %}<main id="main" data-frozen="{{ versionOf }}">{{ versionOf | frozenMain | safe }}</main>{% else %}<main id="main">
{{ content | safe }}
</main>{% endif %}

<footer>
  <div class="sheet">
    <aside class="rail"></aside>
    <div class="foot">
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/oal/">The rubric</a></li>
        <li><a href="/services/">Services</a></li>
        <li><a href="/independence/">Independence</a></li>
        <li><a href="/about/">About</a></li>
        {%- if nav != "scorecard" %}
        <li><a href="/scorecard/">Blank scorecard</a></li>
        {%- endif %}
        {%- if nav != "changelog" %}
        <li><a href="/changelog/">Changelog</a></li>
        {%- endif %}
      </ul>
      {#
        A field list, not a sentence. Terms and Privacy come back as one field each
        once the entity exists, and that has to be a diff rather than a re-layout —
        the same principle the signatory block was built on. Adding a field is one
        line in site.json and moves nothing here.

        The separators are drawn in CSS rather than written into the markup: the
        <ul>/<li> structure already carries the semantics, so the middot is purely
        visual and nothing reads a stray character between fields.
      #}
      <ul class="legal">
        <li>{{ site.name }}</li>
        {%- for field in site.footerFields %}
        <li>{% if field.href %}<a href="{{ field.href }}">{{ field.text | tok }}</a>{% else %}{{ field.text | tok }}{% endif %}</li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</footer>
</body>
</html>
